<accept> The content explicitly states that the "validity of using social media posts for early screening is limited by two factors: (a) the format of the source material does not follow clinical standards that ensure that all symptoms are taken into account; and (2) the ground truth for “mental illness” is disputable". This indicates that reliance on social media posts (a "certain process") limits the ability of automated psychological assessment (NLP models for early screening) to capture deeper insights (ensuring all symptoms are taken into account).</accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'Reliance on static text analysis limits the ability of most existing automated psychological assessment approaches to capture certain insights.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The "Limitations" section of the content states, "As pointed out in our previous research... paraverbal cues (e.g., intonation, speed, stuttering, etc.) and nonverbal cues (e.g., fidgeting, avoiding eye contact, etc.) were either not recorded... or not transcribed and could therefore not be considered in the evaluation." This directly indicates that relying solely on transcribed text (a form of static text analysis) prevents the capture of these specific, valuable insights, thereby limiting the assessment's completeness. </accept>

